Arguments similar to [REF:var_calc] show that for any two edges $e \neq e^\prime$, it holds $n\E(Y_{n,i,e}Y_{n,i,e^\prime}) = O(b_{n,c})$; and with [REF:bias_calc], $n \cov(Y_{n,i,e}, Y_{n,i,e^\prime}) \to 0$. We have shown that [REF:LF2] holds with $\tilde \Sigma_{\bm x}$ being a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $\tilde \sigma_{\bm x, e}$ given in [REF:el_sig].

Instead of checking the remaining condition [REF:LF3] directly, we will verify the stronger Lyapunov-type condition $\sum_{i=1}^n \E(\| \bm Y_{n,i} \|^3 ) \to 0$. By Jensen's inequality we get 
    \begin{align*}
    n\E\bigl(\| \bm Y_{n,1} \|^3 \bigr) = nE\biggl\{\biggl(\sum_{m = 1}^{d - 1}\sum_{e \in E_m}  Y_{n,1,e}^2 \biggr)^{3/2}\biggr\} 
    &\le n\sqrt{d(d-1)/2} \sum_{m = 1}^{d - 1}\sum_{e \in E_m} \E\bigl( Y_{n,1,k}^3 \bigr),
\end{align*}
where $d(d-1)/2$ is the number of terms in the double sum. Hence, it suffices to show $n\E( Y_{n,1,e}^3) \to 0$ for any $e \in E_1, \dots, E_{d-1}$. Similar to [REF:var_calc], we get $n\E( Y_{n,1,k}^3) =  O\{1/(nb_n^2)^{1/2}\}$ which is $o(1)$. $\qed$




